  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "mexican-folk-music-tunes-forms-history-and-recordings",
      "title": "Mexican Folk Music — Tunes — Forms, History, and Recordings",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** Latin American Folk | **Region:** latin-mexico | **Tier:** 4.5\n**Prerequisites:** regions-latin-mexico-07-representative-depth\n**Tags:** latin, mexico, latin-america, tunes, —"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "La Bamba, Cielito Lindo\n\nMexican Folk Music belongs to the **Latin American Folk** curriculum. Vicente Fernández\nLos Folkloristas\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **La Bamba, Cielito Lindo** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-ireland",
      "title": "Compared with Ireland",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "This anchor unit establishes vocabulary for **Latin American Folk**. When you later study sibling nations in this block, compare each lesson to this chapter.\n\n**Optional Atlantic-trad link:** `regions-celtic-ireland-08-tunes` — useful if you completed the Celtic Ireland unit first."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "La Bamba, Cielito Lindo\n\nStudy **mexican folk music** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Music of Mexico](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Mexico) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tune-studies",
      "title": "Tune studies",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Representative repertoire includes **La Bamba, Cielito Lindo**. Use the lesson tune panel and **Play all** to compare multiple settings of the same title or form."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **Latin American Folk**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **violin, voice, harp**. Ensemble layer: **guitar, vihuela, percussion**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **son, huapango, corrido**. Dance context: **zapateado, social dance**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **regional archives**. Contemporary scenes: **mariachi vs village son**. Tune repertoire anchor: **La Bamba, Cielito Lindo**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- La Bamba, Cielito Lindo\n- Lead instruments: violin, voice, harp\n- Ensemble: guitar, vihuela, percussion\n- Forms: son, huapango, corrido\n- Dance: zapateado, social dance\n- History: regional archives\n- Scenes: mariachi vs village son\n- Repertoire: La Bamba, Cielito Lindo\n- Compared with Mexico: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
      "title": "Key points",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Mexican Folk Music is a living tradition with regional accent.\n- Focus: La Bamba, Cielito Lindo.\n- Compared with Mexico: shared heritage, distinct emphasis.\n- Forms: son, huapango, corrido.\n- Dance: zapateado, social dance.\n- Transmission: regional archives."
        }
      ]
    },
